[{"record_id":"rec_000218","title":"Foundation Models for Single-Cell Transcriptomics: A Comparative Analysis of Architectures, Pretraining Strategies, and Empirical Performance","abstract":"The transfer-learning paradigm that reshaped natural language processing and computer vision has, over the past four years, been imported wholesale into single-cell biology. A rapidly growing family of single-cell foundation models (scFMs)-among them scBERT, Geneformer, scGPT, scFoundation, Universal Cell Embeddings, CellPLM, and Nicheformer-pretrain transformer architectures on tens of millions of cells and promise general-purpose, transferable representations of cellular state. Yet these models differ sharply in how they tokenize transcriptomes, in their pretraining objectives, in the scale and composition of their training corpora, and, critically, in the downstream tasks at which they are claimed to excel. This review provides a structured comparative analysis of the scFM landscape. We first establish a common conceptual frameworkinput representation, tokenization, architecture, pretraining objective, and evaluation protocoland then survey the major models along each of these axes, summarizing their design choices in a unified comparison table. We next critically examine the empirical evidence, synthesizing a series of independent benchmarking studies that have found scFMs to match but frequently not exceed well-tuned classical baselines on core tasks such as cell-type annotation, batch integration, and perturbation prediction, especially in zero-shot settings. Finally, we review an emerging line of mechanistic and geometric interpretability work that probes what these models actually encode internally, and argue that representational analysis offers both a more discriminating evaluation lens and a route to extracting useful, lightweight algorithms from large pretrained models. We close by outlining open problems in benchmarking, data curation, multimodality, and biological 