mous combinatorial space of candidate gene-disease pairs and the limited scalability of experimental validation. As wet lab studies cannot keep pace with rapidly expanding omics data, computational approaches have become essential for prioritizing plausible GDAs and accelerating biological discovery. Recent advances in artificial intelligence (AI), particularly graph neural networks (GNNs) and large language models (LLMs), are trans forming this field by enabling richer biological representations and more accurate predictive modeling. In this survey, we provide a unified and up-to-date overview of AI-driven GDA prediction. We first summarize major public resources containing gene, dis ease, and auxiliary biological information that underpin computational studies. We then review methodological developments ranging from traditional network-based methods to machine learning, deep learning, and the emerging integration of GNNs and LLMs, which has received limited attention in previous GDA-focused surveys. Representative applications in gene prioritization, drug repurposing, and clinical research are also discussed to demonstrate the practical impact of these approaches. Finally, we outline current challenges and promising future directions. By integrating data resources, methodological advances, and translational applications, this survey provides a comprehensive overview of modern AI techniques for GDA prediction and aims to support the development of more robust, interpretable, and clinically actionable computational tools. All curated resources and re viewed literature are publicly available in our GitHub repository (last updated September 2025; including peer-reviewed publications and preprints on AI-driven GDA prediction published through September 2025): https://github.com/linyaoyang/gene disease-association-prediction-papers.","doi":"10.1109/tcbbio.2026.3707525","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000222","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract says this is a survey of AI-driven gene-disease association prediction and summarizes prior methods and resources. That makes it a review/resource-style paper rather than a primary candidate model paper.","evidence_snippet":"“In this survey, we provide a unified and up-to-date overview of AI-driven GDA prediction.”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000222","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a survey of AI-driven gene-disease association prediction, not a model paper. The abstract emphasizes predictive modeling and surveys methods, but does not establish a generative architecture or foundation-model characteristics.","evidence_snippet":"\"In this survey, we provide a unified and up-to-date overview of AI-driven GDA prediction\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000223","title":"Advancing Generative Methods for Multimodal Data Analysis","abstract":"… the multimodal fusion of large language model embeddings … highresolution gene expression, enhancing cell-type mapping, … , our model can better predict gene expression patterns by …","doi":"","year":"","venue":"","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000223","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes multimodal fusion of LLM embeddings with gene expression for downstream prediction tasks. That is side-use of external LLM representations rather than a substantive text-bio foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"“multimodal fusion of large language model embeddings” and “predict gene expression patterns”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000223","paper_type":"unclear","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The fragment points to multimodal predictive modeling rather than a clearly generative architecture. It mentions LLM embeddings and gene-expression prediction, but gives no clear evidence of a foundation-model-style pretrained model.","evidence_snippet":"\"multimodal fusion of large language model embeddings\"; \"our model can better predict gene expression patterns\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000225","title":"Leave No Data Behind: Exploring a new paradigm in oncology with foundation models and large language models","abstract":"Foundation models (FMs) and large language models (LLMs) are transforming cancer AI by integrating heterogeneous data sources, including medical imaging, electronic health records, and molecular profiles. By learning from large-scale, unstructured, and label-free inputs, these models may support diagnosis, biomarker discovery, prognostic assessment, treatment personalization, and workflow automation. In this narrative review, we propose the paradigm of \"Leave No Data Behind\" to describe the promise that broad oncology data integration may generate clinically meaningful outputs. We critically assess whether this paradigm is supported by current evidence and identify the key challenges that must be addressed to harness the full potential of FMs and LLMs for clinical implementation in oncology.","doi":"10.1016/j.xcrm.2026.102966","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000225","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly says this is a narrative review of FMs and LLMs in oncology. It discusses multimodal cancer data integration, but the paper itself is not a primary model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"In this narrative review, we propose the paradigm...\" and discusses \"medical imaging, electronic health records, and molecular profiles\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000225","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"Narrative review of FMs/LLMs in oncology; no original model architecture is described.","evidence_snippet":"In this narrative review, we propose the paradigm of Leave No Data Behind"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}}]

